WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.180
Donuts from the sky, slowing down time with cold&nbsp;
brew and a not-so-secret Secret Santa. The rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.671
the episodes are comedy gold, but we're ranking&nbsp;
just the cold opens for "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:00:10.842 --> 00:00:16.305
"Sure, sure, cool, cool, cool, cool, cool."

00:00:16.409 --> 00:00:19.860
Generally, a cold open describes&nbsp;
any episode set-up that takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.740
before the opening title. But&nbsp;
for the purposes of this list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.520
and in accordance with the unique way&nbsp;
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine" handles cold opens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.300
we've defined it as any pre-title skit that&nbsp;
has nothing to do with the rest of the episode.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.200
Obviously, everything on this list&nbsp;
will make you at the very least smile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.400
but there's something particularly special about&nbsp;
this cold open from the episode "Beach House".

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.440
Having detected that Captain Holt may be pantsless&nbsp;
following an uncharacteristic lunch malfunction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.420
Jake decides to provide the squad with visual&nbsp;
proof. Cue several unsuccessful attempts to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.980
the Captain to stand up. Eventually Jake brings&nbsp;
in a replacement bowl of soup to entice him out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.940
of his seat and Holt, of course, wanting&nbsp;
this to end, just admits to sitting in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.500
underwear. Jake celebrates his victory, but&nbsp;
also creates a second soup-pants interaction.

00:01:02.029 --> 00:01:04.985
"Sir I need you to sign off on -"

00:01:04.985 --> 00:01:08.340
"Look at us. Just three people with&nbsp;
pants on having a normal conversation."

00:01:08.511 --> 00:01:10.350
"Yup. No story here."

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.800
While investigating a robbery at an&nbsp;
electronics store in the pilot episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.940
Jake and Amy take very different approaches&nbsp;
to the case: Amy questions the owner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.000
and Jake distracts himself with&nbsp;
anything that happens to catch his eye.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.300
Having arrived early and explaining&nbsp;
that he already found video footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.940
of the robbery thanks to a nanny&nbsp;
cam hidden within a teddy bear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.233
Jake just can't help himself, and makes the&nbsp;
bear hit on Amy, though not successfully.

00:01:33.135 --> 00:01:37.006
"Detective Santiago! Don't walk away from me!"

00:01:37.006 --> 00:01:40.920
This cold open gives viewers a rare&nbsp;
glimpse into the life of one Rosa Diaz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.980
who has never been known to dole out&nbsp;
any semblance of personal information.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.420
Rosa's surprising desire to do something nice&nbsp;
by taking her boyfriend out to celebrate his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.020
birthday spawns all sorts of weird&nbsp;
suggestions, but it's the drunk guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.734
in the holding pen who has the idea Rosa finds&nbsp;
most attractive ... for a few seconds, anyway.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.240
"You can go to the top of the Empire&nbsp;
State Building, that's very romantic."

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.040
"Huh… maybe I will."

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.900
"You can pee on the WHOLE city from up there."

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.740
"Come on!"

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.660
When Charles treats the squad to some&nbsp;
cold brew, he unintentionally causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.260
a caffeine crisis so hilarious, there's&nbsp;
no way it wasn't going to make this list.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.940
Captain Holt, Rosa, and Jake all somehow&nbsp;
forgot that cold coffee still has caffeine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:23.160
in it and went to town on Charles' special brew&nbsp;
and the result is wonderful. From slow-mo Charles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.000
to the double-speed trio, the writing,&nbsp;
editing, and performances are stellar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.353
as the caffeine fiends decide that there's&nbsp;
absolutely nothing wrong with them whatsoever.

00:02:31.353 --> 00:02:31.892
"Captain how do you feel?"

00:02:31.892 --> 00:02:34.740
"Great excellent amazing I feel better than&nbsp;
I've ever felt at any moment in my entire life."

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:35.220
"So we're all fine?"

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.778
[an exorbitant amount of 'Yeps."]

00:02:38.778 --> 00:02:41.760
Jake and Holt's relationship has&nbsp;
always been the lynchpin of the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.240
ranging from their weird father-son&nbsp;
dynamic to the incredible levels of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.380
pettiness they sink to. This particular&nbsp;
cold open falls into the former category.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.240
By Season 6, Holt is getting a little tired of&nbsp;
Jake's issues with tardiness. It's especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.960
galling when one considers the fact that&nbsp;
Jake's paramour is the frickin' queen of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.920
being on time. In order to underline the&nbsp;
importance of timekeeping to his work-son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.560
Captain Holt devises a special kind of torture.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.960
"Six years and no matter how hard I try I still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.700
can't get you to understand the&nbsp;
importance of being punctual."

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.500
"Maybe you should just give up&nbsp;
and accept me for who I am?"

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.480
"No. I will break you. Right now."

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:12.870
"Oh."

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.900
Though he's never been one for any&nbsp;
kind of interpersonal touching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.040
Holt has painstakingly invented and rehearsed&nbsp;
a special, personalized handshake for every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.501
member of the squad, and the copy guy.&nbsp;
It's the one thing Jake never saw coming.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.440
It's no secret that Holt and Amy consider&nbsp;
themselves to be head and shoulders above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.180
the rest of the squad. This superiority reaches&nbsp;
its zenith in the cold open to "Maximum Security."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.740
After Holt gives them a brain teaser that will&nbsp;
reveal the location of a clandestine meeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.780
most of the squad slowly trickles into the broom&nbsp;
closet. Jake is way too full of himself over his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:47.160
victory, but does become suitably embarrassed when&nbsp;
Amy's love of adult puzzle camp is brought up.

00:03:47.314 --> 00:03:50.254
"Doesn't she go to like an&nbsp;
adult puzzle camp every summer?"

00:03:50.254 --> 00:03:53.245
"Yes but please don't bring that up,&nbsp;
I've been throwing out her mailers."

00:03:53.245 --> 00:03:56.460
Perhaps the squad shouldn't be so quick&nbsp;
to mock Amy's summertime activities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.720
however. When they notice that Holt and Amy are&nbsp;
absent, they consider that they might be in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.580
wrong place. Hitchock and Scully's entrance&nbsp;
confirms it. Jake is unable to contain his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.181
disappointment at discovering he's no smarter&nbsp;
than Hitchcock and Scully, and it's beautiful.

00:04:10.181 --> 00:04:14.880
When an incredibly extravagant gift basket&nbsp;
arrives, the squad decides it absolutely must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.440
be for them and they help themselves to the&nbsp;
basket's decidedly decadent contents. Gina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.620
always the wisest member of the team,&nbsp;
interrupts the feast by pointing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.800
that the basket is an obvious gift&nbsp;
to Holt from his husband Kevin.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.480
Panic ensues, but Jake knows exactly how&nbsp;
to fix it. We cut to Captain Holt opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.980
an entirely re-built basket. The squad&nbsp;
waits with bated breath as he inspects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:40.080
replacement contents. A veritable smorgasbord&nbsp;
of shiny new stationery emerges — it's clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.793
the team has spared no expense to cover their&nbsp;
gluttonous tracks, and it works like a charm.

00:04:44.793 --> 00:04:46.858
"...That man really knows me!"

00:04:46.858 --> 00:04:51.900
This cold open has everything that makes&nbsp;
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine" great — Stephanie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.920
Beatriz's complete commitment to deadpan&nbsp;
delivery, which remains intact as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.760
tosses donut holes at Andy Samberg, is&nbsp;
on fire here. With the writers apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.360
unsatisfied with the already brilliant&nbsp;
bit, they take things one step further.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.380
Jake misses catching a donut hole with his&nbsp;
mouth, and it goes sailing off the roof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.740
but it's only natural that the stray&nbsp;
pastry finds a home in Scully's mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.180
several floors below. Yup, Rosa is that&nbsp;
good, and fried desserts falling from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.480
the sky is literally something that&nbsp;
must be a dream come true for Scully.

00:05:19.146 --> 00:05:21.600
"It's finally happening!"

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.560
We're used to seeing Hitchcock bring pain&nbsp;
entirely on himself, but in this skit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.680
he's merely the innocent victim of&nbsp;
Jake's diabolical scheme to make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.000
older detective pee his pants. Well,&nbsp;
as innocent as Hitchcock gets, anyway.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.100
Jake relies on the tried-and-true summer camp&nbsp;
method for his prank, leaving Hitchcock's hand in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:43.020
a bowl of lukewarm water. Jake immediately brags&nbsp;
about his joke to the squad. When they check on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:47.640
the pee progress, Hitchcock is somehow facedown&nbsp;
in the water. They grab him in the nick of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.935
but in Rosa's opinion, this macabre turn&nbsp;
hugely improves the whole situation.

00:05:52.079 --> 00:05:53.923
"I take it back, Jake, great prank."

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:59.520
When Hitchcock feels he's been badly treated at&nbsp;
the local coffee shop, he vents his frustrations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.660
to the gathered squad members. He's about&nbsp;
as worked up as we ever see him, so clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.140
the slight has had a profound effect.&nbsp;
Who knew Hitchcock was so concerned with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:10.440
how people see him? Maybe there's a soft&nbsp;
side to him that we haven't seen before?

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.000
Nope. Jake eventually interrupts Hitchcock&nbsp;
to tell him the reason for the odd treatment.

00:06:15.204 --> 00:06:17.034
"Hey Hitchcock, your penis is hanging out."

00:06:17.649 --> 00:06:19.015
"Oh, well that's a relief."

00:06:19.015 --> 00:06:24.180
When Amy fails to appear at her desk at exactly&nbsp;
9 a.m, everyone comes up with outlandish theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.920
to explain her uncharacteristic tardiness.&nbsp;
Holt's contribution, however, clearly pushes his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.300
imagination to its very limits. Having searched&nbsp;
his mind for the ultimate bizarre scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.320
he proudly proclaims his guess that she's&nbsp;
running late because of a long line at the bank.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.060
Andre Braugher's deadpan delivery is amazing,&nbsp;
but he's actually saving the best for last.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.020
When Amy walks in seconds later, the&nbsp;
squad demands that she explain herself.

00:06:46.191 --> 00:06:48.146
"There was a problem at the bank…"

00:06:48.146 --> 00:06:49.304
"Hot damn!"

00:06:49.304 --> 00:06:54.000
Each member of the team picks a name for Secret&nbsp;
Santa in this holiday-themed cold open — except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.860
for Rosa, who refuses to participate. What&nbsp;
follows belongs entirely to Andy Samberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.720
thanks to his absolutely perfect delivery. Almost&nbsp;
instantly identifying everyone's Secret Santa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.680
Jake proceeds to humble-brag about his&nbsp;
skills. We even discover he's legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.040
changed his middle name to Sherlock,&nbsp;
for exactly this sort of situation.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.760
Rather than being impressed by Jake's&nbsp;
antics, Holt speaks for the whole squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:18.000
and suggests something that shows their&nbsp;
displeasure at Jake ruining the game.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.340
"...Should we draw the names&nbsp;
again and leave Jake out?"

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:20.840
"Yeah!"

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.800
"Nooo! Sherlock wants a present!"

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.480
There has never been, nor will there ever&nbsp;
be, a better cold open than this. As Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.500
and a haunted-looking woman examine a lineup&nbsp;
of five men, she reveals that while she didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.880
get a look at the criminal's face, she heard&nbsp;
him singing the Backstreet Boys' 1999 hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:41.040
"I Want It That Way." Jake has them start singing,&nbsp;
and soon it becomes a full blown karaoke session.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.300
The idea of having a police lineup sing&nbsp;
"I Want It That Way" might sound surreal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.340
but in practice, it's effortlessly perfect.&nbsp;
The musical magic is so powerful, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.442
that Jake joins in and forgets&nbsp;
the reason for the singing.

00:07:53.442 --> 00:07:55.980
"It was number five. Number&nbsp;
five killed my brother."

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:57.600
"Oh my God. I forgot about that part."

